findings.claim:
The cookie attribute set is a rule each call site has to remember, and there are at least five of them:
- set the OAuth cookie at login;
- clear it on every callback outcome, which is many branches;
- set the session cookie;
- expire it at logout;
- any later cookie.

Browsers reject every Set-Cookie for a __Host- name that lacks Secure or Path=/, and that includes a deletion. So a clear written as {Name, MaxAge:-1} is silently ignored in production. The OAuth cookie then survives the callback (the design leans on 'cleared on every callback outcome' for replay), and logout leaves the sid cookie in the browser.

The tests cannot see this:
- The end-to-end tests run on loopback http with the plain names, where no prefix rule applies.
- TestCookieAttributes is specified only for the set path.

The spike's 'set Lax explicitly' constraint is likewise not enforced for future cookies: the Strict rule does not catch an unset SameSite.
